      standfirst: 'Choosing a direction creates a commitment. Holding it means giving the business a practical way to use that commitment when circumstances change.',
      date: '2026-09-09',
      sections: [
        { heading: 'The next request is the real test', paragraphs: [
          'A direction can appear settled at the end of a presentation. Then a new opportunity arrives. A prospective customer wants an adjacent service. Someone proposes a campaign with a different tone. A new colleague interprets the promise differently. Each request is reasonable in isolation. The business needs a way to judge what those requests would mean together.',
          'I call this movement Hold within The Collapse. It gives a decision continuity after Observe, Collapse and Build have made it concrete. The work concerns everyday interpretation: how the chosen direction helps someone answer the next question. A decision gains practical strength when people can use it without needing its original author present for every conversation.'
        ] },
        { heading: 'Preserve the reason for the choice', paragraphs: [
          'Guidelines can describe a visual expression while leaving the underlying judgement inaccessible. I want the business to retain the reason for the direction: the situation it addressed, the audience it prioritised, the tradeoff it accepted and the assumptions it relied upon. That record gives a new colleague more than instructions. It gives them a basis for making a coherent choice.',
          'The record should be brief enough to consult during work. A long archive is useful for reference, but the active decision needs a usable form. Someone should be able to explain what the company is choosing to be known for, what supports that choice and which opportunities deserve greater scrutiny because they pull the business elsewhere.'
        ] },
        { heading: 'Give interpretation an owner', paragraphs: [
          'A direction belongs to the business, but interpretation needs a clear point of responsibility. Otherwise, every disagreement can become a fresh vote on the whole strategy. I want an owner who can hear a request, understand the reasoning and decide whether it fits, needs adaptation or raises a larger question. That responsibility should be visible to the people doing the work.',
          'Ownership also requires a path for useful disagreement. A sales conversation may reveal a question the existing language handles poorly. A product change may expose a limitation in the offer structure. The person closest to that evidence needs a way to bring it forward. A held decision becomes stronger when relevant observations can reach someone equipped to act on them.'
        ] },
        { heading: 'Distinguish learning from restlessness', paragraphs: [
          'I would separate a request to improve the expression from a reason to reconsider the commitment. A sentence can become clearer while the position stays sound. A page can be reorganised while the intended buyer remains the same. Treating every revision as a strategic reopening burdens ordinary work with decisions that have already been made.',
          'A deeper change deserves a deeper argument. Has the business altered what it can deliver? Has the intended audience changed? Has evidence challenged an assumption on which the direction depends? These questions create a basis for revision. Personal fatigue with a visual system may be real, but it deserves to be examined alongside the experience of the people encountering it.'
        ] },
        { heading: 'Design the return to observation', paragraphs: [
          'Hold includes a route back to Observe. I want the client and me to agree what should be watched, who will interpret it and when the direction deserves a deliberate review. The rhythm should fit the business and the pace of meaningful change. Its purpose is to create attention at the right moments while giving daily work a stable basis.',
          'That is why I treat the period after launch as part of the original design problem. A direction needs a body, an owner and a memory. It also needs permission to evolve when the argument changes. Holding a decision means keeping its purpose active long enough to learn from it, with a clear way to decide what should happen next.'
        ] }
      ]
